{"name":"Spring Reflection","hex":"#a1bfab","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spring","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or burst forth.","To appear."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of springing: a leap, a jump.","The season of the year in temperate regions in which temperatures and daylight hours rise, and plants spring from the ground and into bloom and dormant animals spring to life."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spring"},{"term":"reflection","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of reflecting or the state of being reflected.","The property of a propagated wave being thrown back from a surface (such as a mirror)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reflection"}],"entries":[{"title":"Reflection (physics)","blocks":[{"text":"Reflection is the change in direction of a wavefront at an interface between two different media so that the wavefront returns into the medium from which it originated. Common examples include the reflection of light, sound and water waves. The law of reflection says that for specular reflection the angle at which the wave is incident on the surface equals the angle at which it is reflected."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg/330px-Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)","wikidata":"Q165939"},{"title":"Reflection (mathematics)","blocks":[{"text":"In mathematics, a reflection is a mapping from a Euclidean space to itself that is an isometry with a hyperplane as the set of fixed points; this set is called the axis or plane of reflection. The image of a figure by a reflection is its mirror image in the axis or plane of reflection."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/SimmetriainvOK.svg/330px-SimmetriainvOK.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(mathematics)","wikidata":"Q426221"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketches of specular, spread, diffuse, and compound reflection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/500px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/1280px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/500px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An example of the law of reflection","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AF%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Studio_soundproofing_panel.jpg/500px-Studio_soundproofing_panel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/